# TTOS CTF bench rig -- pin the CAN adapters to ROLE names.
#
# WHY ROLE NAMES AND NOT can0/can1:
# A test that says "observe ttdiag" cannot be silently backwards, whatever the
# cabling happens to be.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CURRENT HARDWARE (2026-08-04)
#
#   DUT      one PCAN-USB Pro FD, DUAL channel, on arcana port 3-6.4
#              channel 0 (dev_id 0x0) -> DUT candiag   (DIAG bus)
#              channel 1 (dev_id 0x1) -> DUT candrive  (DRIVE bus)
#   car 02   one PCAN-USB FD, single channel, on the other USB controller
#              -> the vehicle's diagnostic port
#
# The DUT rig is for unit and functional work against emulated nodes; a real car
# is the only place the real firmware, drivetrain and bus timing exist.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY dev_id AND NOT ID_PATH
#
# This replaced two single-channel dongles on 2026-08-04, and the change breaks the
# old pinning strategy outright: BOTH CHANNELS OF THE PRO FD SHARE ONE ID_PATH.
# They are one USB interface --
#
#   ttdiag  ID_PATH=pci-0000:06:00.3-usb-0:6.4:1.0
#   can1    (same DEVPATH: .../3-6.4:1.0/net/...)
#
# -- so a port-based rule cannot tell them apart. It matched whichever channel
# probed first and left the other to the kernel's can0/can1, which is exactly the
# probe-order race this project already fought on the Pi.
#
# peak_usb/can_channel_id is NOT usable either: it reads 80FF0000 on both.
#
# ATTR{dev_id} is the channel index within the device -- 0x0 and 0x1, fixed by
# which physical connector a cable is in. That is the discriminator.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOW THE MAPPING WAS ESTABLISHED -- by traffic, in both directions
#
#   dev_id 0x1  saw the DUT's 0x100 heartbeat (50 frames in 10 s). Only the DRIVE
#               bus carries it; the gateway does not forward it to DIAG.
#   dev_id 0x0  saw NOTHING -- which alone proves nothing, because a silent bus and
#               an unplugged cable look identical. POSITIVE CONTROL: a UDS VIN read
#               on it was answered (TTKTREK13TC000001), so it is genuinely the DIAG
#               bus and genuinely connected.
#
# Never establish this mapping by reasoning about the other end. A bus-role
# correction on the DUT is not a correction here -- conflating those inverted every
# bench observation for a day in August 2026, and nothing failed loudly, because a
# swapped pair makes negative assertions pass for free.
#
# bench-up.sh re-verifies against the live 0x100 heartbeat on every run, so a
# mis-plug aborts before any assertion runs rather than inverting its result. RUN IT
# after touching anything physical.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOTE ON SWAPPING ROLES: udev cannot rename two interfaces into each other's names
# -- the first rename fails because the target name is still taken, and it fails
# SILENTLY, leaving the old mapping in place. If you invert these lines, rename
# through a temporary name by hand or reboot. That bit once already.
#
# Install:  sudo cp 99-ttos-bench-can.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/
#           sudo udevadm control --reload && sudo udevadm trigger -s net

# DUT -- both channels of the dual adapter, told apart by channel index.
SUBSYSTEM=="net", ACTION=="add", DRIVERS=="peak_usb", ENV{ID_PATH}=="pci-0000:06:00.3-usb-0:6.4:1.0", ATTR{dev_id}=="0x0", NAME="ttdiag"
SUBSYSTEM=="net", ACTION=="add", DRIVERS=="peak_usb", ENV{ID_PATH}=="pci-0000:06:00.3-usb-0:6.4:1.0", ATTR{dev_id}=="0x1", NAME="ttdrive"

# A whole vehicle's diagnostic port, on the other USB controller.
SUBSYSTEM=="net", ACTION=="add", ENV{ID_PATH}=="pci-0000:0a:00.3-usb-0:3:1.0", NAME="ttcardiag"
